.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt VIS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vis ,
.Nm nvis ,
.Nm strvis ,
.Nm strnvis ,
.Nm strvisx ,
.Nm strnvisx
.Nd visually encode bytes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In vis.h
.Ft char *
.Fn vis "char *dst" "int c" "int flags" "int nextc"
.Ft char *
.Fn nvis "char *dst" "size_t dlen" "int c" "int flags" "int nextc"
.Ft int
.Fn strvis "char *dst" "const char *src" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn strnvis "char *dst" "size_t dlen" "const char *src" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn strvisx "char *dst" "const char *src" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn strnvisx "char *dst" "size_t dlen" "const char *src" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm vis
family renders selected bytes with printable escape forms.
One input byte produces between one and four output bytes, followed by a
terminating NUL.
The
.Fn vis
and
.Fn nvis
functions encode
.Fa c ;
.Fa nextc
selects the unambiguous C-style encoding for a NUL before an octal digit.
.Pp
The
.Fn strvis
and
.Fn strnvis
functions encode bytes through the first NUL in
.Fa src .
The
.Fn strvisx
and
.Fn strnvisx
functions encode exactly
.Fa len
bytes, including embedded NUL bytes.
.Pp
The functions whose names contain
.Sq n
treat
.Fa dlen
as the complete destination size, including the terminating NUL.
They return an error unless the complete encoding and terminator fit.
An insufficient destination remains unchanged, so the result never ends with
part of an encoded byte.
The historical unbounded functions require the caller to provide four times
the input length plus one byte.
.Pp
The following values may be ORed into
.Fa flags :
.Bl -tag -width VIS_NOSLASH
.It Dv VIS_OCTAL
Use the four-byte
.Sq \eDDD
form for every encoded byte.
.It Dv VIS_CSTYLE
Use C escapes such as
.Sq \en ,
.Sq \et ,
and
.Sq \e0
where they apply.
.It Dv VIS_SP , VIS_TAB , VIS_NL
Encode space, tab, or newline respectively.
.Dv VIS_WHITE
selects all three.
.It Dv VIS_SAFE
Copy backspace, bell, and carriage return without encoding them.
.It Dv VIS_NOSLASH
Omit the leading backslash from the meta and control forms.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn vis
and
.Fn nvis
return a pointer to the terminating NUL.
.Fn strvis ,
.Fn strnvis ,
.Fn strvisx ,
and
.Fn strnvisx
return the encoded length without the terminating NUL.
.Pp
On failure,
.Fn nvis
returns
.Dv NULL ;
.Fn strnvis
and
.Fn strnvisx
return -1.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The destination cannot hold the complete encoding and its terminating NUL.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr unvis 3
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn vis ,
.Fn strvis ,
and
.Fn strvisx
derive from 4.4BSD.
The bounded interfaces follow the contracts introduced in
.Nx 6 .
